Key Libraries and Tools
Data Storage
CSV (Comma-Separated Values): A simple, plain-text file format for tabular data[1]. In this project, a CSV file stores the list of courses (with columns like title, category, etc.). It is used by the recommendation engine (via pandas) and the chatbot for reading course data. CSV files are easy to read and edit in spreadsheets or text editors. Their benefit is simplicity and portability: you can quickly open or share course data in CSV form, and pandas can load it directly into a DataFrame for processing[2][1].
SQLite: A lightweight, file-based SQL database. Python’s built-in sqlite3 module provides a straightforward interface to read and write SQLite databases[3]. We can store the same course data in an SQLite database as an alternative to CSV. In our system, the recommendation engine or UI code can query the SQLite file (e.g. filtering courses by category) without loading all data at once. Using SQLite is beneficial when the dataset grows larger or when we want fast queries: it allows efficient selection of rows and columns (like “SELECT * WHERE category=…”) and can outperform CSV for large data[4]. (Pandas can also query SQLite via read_sql if needed.)
Data Processing Libraries
Pandas: A powerful open-source data analysis library[2]. It provides the DataFrame object for loading and manipulating tabular data in Python. In our project, Pandas is used by the recommendation engine (and possibly the UI) to load the local course data (from CSV or SQLite) and prepare it for the model. For example, Pandas can filter courses by features, merge tables of user preferences, or convert a database query into a table. This makes it easy to clean and transform data (handle missing values, sort or group data) before feeding it to the recommendation algorithm. Pandas is needed because it greatly simplifies data handling tasks (sorting, grouping, filtering, joining columns), which would be cumbersome with plain Python lists or CSV parsing[2].
NumPy: A fundamental package for scientific computing in Python[5]. It provides the ndarray, a fast N-dimensional array object, along with optimized mathematical functions. In our recommender, NumPy is used (often under the hood) to perform numerical computations on data matrices – for example, converting feature columns into numeric arrays, computing similarity scores, or performing linear algebra. NumPy arrays are much faster and more memory-efficient than regular Python lists for large numerical data. This speed and efficiency benefit the model’s calculations (e.g. multiplying vectors or normalizing data), enabling the recommendation engine to run quickly on local hardware[5]. (Scikit-learn also uses NumPy internally.)
Recommendation Engine
scikit-learn: A widely-used open-source machine learning library for Python[6]. It provides a large collection of standard algorithms (classification, regression, clustering, etc.) and tools for model building. In the recommendation engine, scikit-learn is used to build and evaluate the recommendation model. For example, one might use scikit-learn’s nearest-neighbors, matrix factorization, or feature extraction (TF-IDF) classes to compute course similarities or predict course scores. Scikit-learn is used specifically in the backend engine; it handles the “learning” and prediction steps (like calling .fit() on training data and .predict() for recommendations). The benefit of scikit-learn is that it offers ready-made, well-tested algorithms and utilities (like train/test splitting, pipelines, cross-validation) so we don’t have to implement models from scratch. It makes it easy to prototype, tune, and maintain the machine learning parts of the recommender[6].
Frontend/UI Frameworks
Streamlit: An open-source Python framework for building interactive web apps[7]. It lets you turn a Python script into a web interface with a few simple commands. In this project, Streamlit would be used for the frontend/user interface: for example, to create forms or sliders where a user selects their interests or enters preferences, and to display the list of recommended courses and the chatbot interface. Streamlit is needed because it greatly simplifies UI development. You can write widgets (buttons, text inputs, etc.) directly in Python and Streamlit handles the browser layout – no HTML/CSS/JavaScript is needed[7]. This allows data scientists to deploy a user-friendly app quickly and iteratively. Streamlit also automatically updates the UI when the code changes, which speeds up development.
Gradio: Another open-source Python library for building web interfaces for machine learning models[8]. Like Streamlit, Gradio provides pre-built components (text boxes, dropdowns, chat interfaces, etc.) so you can create an interactive app in Python with minimal code. In our system, Gradio is an alternative to Streamlit for the frontend. Using Gradio, we could quickly assemble a UI (e.g. a chat window and input forms) and host it locally or on Hugging Face Spaces. Gradio is needed because it “handles the frontend” while you focus on model logic[9] – it takes care of serving the app in the browser. This makes it easy to share the recommender or chatbot with others, without building a web frontend from scratch.
Chatbot (Local AI) Tools
Ollama: An open-source platform (and command-line tool/SDK) for running large language models (LLMs) locally[10]. Ollama lets you download and serve models on your own machine. In our project, Ollama provides the backend for the offline chatbot. We install Ollama and use its Python SDK (pip install ollama) in the chatbot code. When the user types a question, the Python code calls the Ollama service to run the Phi-3 model on that input. Ollama is needed because it makes it easy to use cutting-edge LLMs without internet access or paid APIs. It improves privacy (no data leaves your machine) and cost (no API charges). As one source explains, “Integrating local LLMs with Ollama is a great strategy for improving privacy, reducing costs, and building offline-capable AI-powered apps”[11]. Ollama abstracts the complexities of managing the model process and lets the Python chatbot code focus on sending prompts and receiving replies.
Phi-3 (LLM model): Phi-3 is a state-of-the-art open-source language model developed by Microsoft[12]. It comes in different sizes (e.g. 3B “Mini” and 14B “Medium” parameters) and is tuned for chat and reasoning tasks. In our offline chatbot, Phi-3 is the actual AI model that generates responses. We “run” Phi-3 using Ollama on the local machine. The Phi-3 model takes the user’s input and produces a text reply (for example, answering questions about courses or giving study advice). Using Phi-3 provides powerful language understanding and generation capabilities without relying on an online service. The benefit of using Phi-3 (with Ollama) is that we get a modern conversational AI engine that works completely offline – offering fast, context-aware replies to the user while keeping all processing local[12][10].
Each of these tools plays a clear role: Data (CSV/SQLite) is stored and accessed; Pandas/NumPy prepare that data numerically; scikit-learn builds the recommendation logic; Streamlit/Gradio present inputs and results to the user; and Ollama+Phi-3 power the offline AI chatbot. Together, they form a complete, beginner-friendly Python stack for a local course recommendation system with an integrated AI assistant.
